{"name":"Reservation","hex":"#8c7544","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"reservation","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The act of reserving, withholding or keeping back.","The practice of reserving part of the consecrated bread of the Eucharist for the communion of the sick."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/reservation"}],"entries":[{"title":"Indian reservation","blocks":[{"text":"An Indian reservation in the United States is an area of land held and governed by a Native American tribal nation officially recognized by the U.S. federal government. The reservation's government is autonomous but subject to regulations passed by the United States Congress, and is administered by the United States Bureau of Indian Affairs. It is not subject however, to a state or local government of the U.S. state in which it is located. Some of the country's 574 federally recognized tribes govern more than one of the 326 Indian reservations in the United States, while some share reservations, and others have no reservation at all. Historical piecemeal land allocations under the Dawes Act facilitated sales to non–Native Americans, resulting in some reservations becoming severely fragmented, with pieces of tribal and privately held land being treated as separate enclaves. This intersection of private and public real estate creates significant administrative, political, and legal difficulties."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Indian_Reservations.png/330px-Indian_Reservations.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indian_reservation"},{"title":"Military base","blocks":[{"text":"A military base is a facility directly owned and operated by or for the military or one of its branches that shelters military equipment and personnel, and facilitates training and operations. A military base always provides accommodations for one or more units, but it may also be used as a command center, training ground or proving ground. In most cases, military bases rely on outside help to operate. However, certain complex bases are able to endure on their own for long periods because they are able to provide food, drinking water, and other necessities for their inhabitants while under siege. Bases for military aviation are called air bases. Bases for military ships are called naval bases."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Military_base"},{"title":"Reservation (law)","blocks":[{"text":"A reservation in international law is a caveat to a state's acceptance of a treaty. A reservation is defined by the 1969 Vienna Convention on the Law of Treaties (VCLT) as:"},{"text":"a unilateral statement, however phrased or named, made by a State, when signing, ratifying, accepting, approving or acceding to a treaty, whereby it purports to exclude or to modify the legal effect of certain provisions of the treaty in their application to that State."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reservation_(law)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Indian_Tribes_and_Linguistic_Stocks%2C_1650.jpg/500px-Indian_Tribes_and_Linguistic_Stocks%2C_1650.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Indian_Tribes_and_Linguistic_Stocks%2C_1650.jpg/1280px-Indian_Tribes_and_Linguistic_Stocks%2C_1650.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Indian_Tribes_and_Linguistic_Stocks%2C_1650.jpg/500px-Indian_Tribes_and_Linguistic_Stocks%2C_1650.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Indian (Native American) tribes and linguistic stocks in 1650.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIndian_Tribes_and_Linguistic_Stocks%2C_1650.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/British_colonies_1763-76_shepherd1923.PNG/500px-British_colonies_1763-76_shepherd1923.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/British_colonies_1763-76_shepherd1923.PNG/1280px-British_colonies_1763-76_shepherd1923.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/British_colonies_1763-76_shepherd1923.PNG/500px-British_colonies_1763-76_shepherd1923.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The British colonies in North America from 1763 to 1775, at the outbreak of the American Revolutionary War, including the locations of Indian Reserve (highlighted in grey) and the proposed colonies of Charlotiana, Transylvania, and Vandalia","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABritish_colonies_1763-76_shepherd1923.PNG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Indian_Country-Territory_1834.jpg/500px-Indian_Country-Territory_1834.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Indian_Country-Territory_1834.jpg/500px-Indian_Country-Territory_1834.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Indian Country (highlighted in red) in 1834","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIndian_Country-Territory_1834.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/New-mexico-rez.jpg/500px-New-mexico-rez.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/New-mexico-rez.jpg/1280px-New-mexico-rez.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/New-mexico-rez.jpg/500px-New-mexico-rez.